Below is a **stand‑alone C# console‑app** that demonstrates the basics of Entity Framework Core (EF Core).  
It shows

| What it covers | Why it matters |
|----------------|----------------|
| Defining POCO entities (`Blog`, `Post`) | The objects that EF maps to tables |
| Creating a `DbContext` (`BloggingContext`) | The gateway to the database |
| Configuring relationships in `OnModelCreating` | EF‑Core’s Fluent API |
| Using an **In‑Memory** database (so no external server is required) | Great for demos, tests, and quick prototyping |
| Performing CRUD operations with `async` EF‑Core methods | Typical usage pattern |
| Dependency‑Injection (DI) with `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` | Modern .NET style |

> **Prerequisites**  
> Add the following NuGet packages to the project (or use the dotnet‑cli):
> ```bash
> dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore
> dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory
> dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection
> ```

```csharp
// Program.cs
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

namespace EfCoreDemo
{
    /* --------------------------------------------------------------
       1. POCO entities ------------------------------------------------*/
    public class Blog
    {
        public int BlogId { get; set; }          // PK
        public string Url { get; set; }

        // Navigation property – one Blog has many Posts
        public List<Post> Posts { get; set; } = new();
    }

    public class Post
    {
        public int PostId { get; set; }          // PK
        public string Title { get; set; }
        public string Content { get; set; }

        // FK to Blog
        public int BlogId { get; set; }
        public Blog Blog { get; set; }
    }

    /* --------------------------------------------------------------
       2. DbContext -----------------------------------------------------*/
    public class BloggingContext : DbContext
    {
        public BloggingContext(DbContextOptions<BloggingContext> options)
            : base(options) { }

        public DbSet<Blog> Blogs => Set<Blog>();
        public DbSet<Post> Posts => Set<Post>();

        protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
        {
            // Configure the one‑to‑many relationship
            modelBuilder.Entity<Blog>()
                .HasMany(b => b.Posts)
                .WithOne(p => p.Blog)
                .HasForeignKey(p => p.BlogId);
        }
    }

    /* --------------------------------------------------------------
       3. Application entry point --------------------------------------*/
    class Program
    {
        static async Task Main()
        {
            /* -----------------------------
               3.1. Configure DI & EF Core  */
            var services = new ServiceCollection();
            services.AddDbContext<BloggingContext>(options =>
                options.UseInMemoryDatabase("BloggingDemoDb")); // In‑memory store
            var serviceProvider = services.BuildServiceProvider();

            /* -----------------------------
               3.2. Get an instance of the context */
            await using var scope = serviceProvider.CreateAsyncScope();
            var context = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<BloggingContext>();

            /* ------------------------------------------------------------
               4. CRUD operations -------------------------------------------*/
            // CREATE ------------------------------------------------------
            var newBlog = new Blog { Url = "https://firstblog.com" };
            context.Blogs.Add(newBlog);
            await context.SaveChangesAsync(); // Persist to the in‑memory DB

            // READ -------------------------------------------------------
            var blogs = await context.Blogs
                .Include(b => b.Posts)          // Eager load Posts
                .ToListAsync();

            Console.WriteLine("Blogs after creation:");
            foreach (var b in blogs)
                Console.WriteLine($" - {b.BlogId}: {b.Url} (Posts: {b.Posts.Count})");

            // UPDATE ------------------------------------------------------
            var blogToUpdate = blogs.First();
            blogToUpdate.Url = "https://updatedblog.com";
            await context.SaveChangesAsync();

            // DELETE ------------------------------------------------------
            context.Blogs.Remove(blogToUpdate);
            await context.SaveChangesAsync();

            Console.WriteLine("\nBlogs after deletion:");
            var remainingBlogs = await context.Blogs.ToListAsync();
            foreach (var b in remainingBlogs)
                Console.WriteLine($" - {b.BlogId}: {b.Url}");

            Console.WriteLine("\nDemo completed. Press any key to exit.");
            Console.ReadKey();
        }
    }
}
```

### What this code does

| Step | Action | EF Core API |
|------|--------|-------------|
| 1 | Define `Blog` & `Post` classes | Plain C# objects (POCOs) |
| 2 | Create `BloggingContext` | Derives from `DbContext`, holds `DbSet<T>`s |
| 3 | Configure relationship in `OnModelCreating` | Fluent API: `HasMany/WithOne` |
| 4 | Register context with DI & use `UseInMemoryDatabase` | Enables testing/quick demo without SQL Server |
| 5 | `context.Blogs.Add(...)` | Add entity to context |
| 6 | `context.SaveChangesAsync()` | Persist changes |
| 7 | `context.Blogs.Include(b => b.Posts).ToListAsync()` | Read data + eager load navigation |
| 8 | `context.Blogs.Remove(blog)` | Delete entity |
| 9 | `await context.SaveChangesAsync()` | Commit delete |

Feel free to replace `UseInMemoryDatabase` with another provider (`UseSqlServer`, `UseSqlite`, etc.) and add migrations (`dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate && dotnet ef database update`) when you move beyond a demo. Happy coding!
